data: {"type":"response.function_call_arguments.done","sequence_number":1229,"item_id":"fc_03aa7dcbe58605d2016adb3e05ee308191a19b09bb73d41bca","output_index":3,"arguments":"{\"artifact\":{\"inference\":\"R001\",\"packet_sha256\":\"fbcc7e27449dbfa9f3cea8738fda78ba5a150dfb49c189c914c1246b049ec7a8\",\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"Fresh review boundary: the complete supplied packet.json and the declared Yui Audit A, self-containment, and format rules. I treated N001–N004 as allowed conditional premises and did not use source proofs, ancestor proofs, or ambient assumptions. This review does not authenticate carrier identity, canonical hash binding, or transmitted-material readback. First unsupported implication: in S002, N004's pointwise vanishing of the integrand is asserted to imply that its Lebesgue integral is zero. That requires the integral-zero property (and the relevant integral interpretation), which is neither a direct input nor an actually established local fact; the packet's only explicit calculation policy is finite-dimensional differentiation and inner-product identities. Thus the supplied proof is not sufficient from this packet alone. No repair or alternative proof is supplied.\",\"checks\":{\"inputs\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"N001–N004 are exactly the listed direct inputs to R001. Under the requested conditional-premise boundary, their statements may be used as given; no source-fidelity judgment is made.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/inputs\",\"/inputs\"]},\"argument\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"S001's extraction of integrability and differentiability from N001–N002 is direct. The first unsupported step is S002: pointwise zero of the integrand (N004) is said to entail zero of its Lebesgue integral. This integral property is not proved locally or listed as an input, and is outside the packet's stated finite-dimensional calculation allowance.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/1\",\"/inference/argument/0\",\"/inputs/3\"]},\"dependency_accounting\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"N001–N004 each have identifiable roles, but S002 additionally relies on the unlisted fact that an identically zero integrand has integral zero. The input inventory and uses metadata do not account for or establish that fact.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/inputs\",\"/inference/argument/1/uses\",\"/inference/argument/1/text\"]},\"output\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The stated goal for arbitrary positive r is exactly the output P: integrability, differentiability, and E'(r)=0. The first two clauses follow directly from N001 and N002; the third is blocked by the argument gap.\",\"locations\":[\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/0\",\"/inference/argument/1\"]},\"scope\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The arbitrary positive r is introduced before use, retained through both steps, and universally generalized to the for-every-r output. No witness or temporary assumption escapes scope.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/locals/0\",\"/inference/argument/0\",\"/inference/argument/1\",\"/output/statement\"]},\"applicability\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"R001 applies no E/M source theorem; the listed N-inputs are used with matching positive-r and pointwise/integral statements. Conditional input use is within the specified boundary.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/inputs\",\"/inference/argument/0/uses\",\"/inference/argument/1/uses\",\"/inference/applications\"]},\"statement_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The direct input and output statements provide their mathematical content in full for the uses made here; S002 identifies the relevant integrand, and no theorem name or external equation reference substitutes for a statement.\",\"locations\":[\"/inputs/0/statement\",\"/inputs/1/statement\",\"/inputs/2/statement\",\"/inputs/3/statement\",\"/output/statement\",\"/inference/argument/1/text\"]},\"object_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"PASS\",\"notes\":\"The objects used in the inference are declared by packet parameters, the D definitions, input-statement binders, or the local arbitrary-r declaration. The integral variable x is bound in the displayed integral/pointwise input statements; no undeclared witness is used.\",\"locations\":[\"/parameters\",\"/definitions\",\"/inputs\",\"/inference/locals/0\",\"/inference/argument\"]},\"dependency_closure\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"Actual factual uses mostly map to listed inputs, but S002's pointwise-zero-to-zero-integral implication has no direct input or prior local proof. Therefore the inference's factual dependency closure is incomplete.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/1/text\",\"/inference/argument/1/uses_local\",\"/inputs/3/statement\"]},\"packet_replay\":{\"verdict\":\"FAIL\",\"notes\":\"On the supplied packet alone, the claimed conclusion is not established: the first missing justification is the Lebesgue-integral step in S002. I read the complete packet and relevant declared rules; I used no other mathematical task material. This reports mathematical sufficiency only and makes no runtime/carrier or transmitted-material attestation.\",\"locations\":[\"/inference/argument/1/text\",\"/logic/calculations\",\"/warning\"]}},\"findings\":[{\"severity\":\"blocking\",\"location\":\"/inference/argument/1/text\",\"premises\":\"N004: for every x, u_t(x,r)·(u_tt(x,r)−Δ_xu(x,r))=0; N003 identifies E'(r) with twice the Lebesgue integral of that expression.\",\"conclusion\":\"The integral in N003 is zero, hence E'(r)=0.\",\"reason\":\"The inference from pointwise vanishing to zero Lebesgue integral is not a listed premise and is not derived locally. The declared calculation allowance does not include an integral property. This is the first unsupported implication; descendants depending on E'(r)=0 are blocked.\"}]}}"}

